/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/sc-300.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("sc-300", [["sc1",0,1,"Your company bought the domain contoso.com and wants users to sign in as name@contoso.com. You added the domain in Microsoft Entra ID. What must you do before you can set it as users' UPN suffix?",["Buy a public TLS certificate for contoso.com and upload it to the tenant","Run Microsoft Entra Connect with the domain filtering option","Create a TXT or MX record the admin center gives you and verify it","Open a support request so Microsoft can transfer ownership"],2,"A custom domain must be verified by adding the TXT (or MX) record Microsoft gives you to public DNS. Certificates and sync tools are not part of domain verification.","Configure and manage custom domains",["TLS certificates secure web traffic for a site; Entra ID never asks for one to prove you own a domain.","Entra Connect syncs on-premises AD objects; domain filtering chooses what to sync and doesn't verify domain ownership.",null,"Microsoft doesn't transfer domain ownership by support ticket; you prove ownership yourself through a DNS record."],null],["sc2",0,1,"Regional IT staff in Germany must reset passwords only for German users. Which approach follows least privilege?",["Assign Helpdesk Administrator scoped to a Germany administrative unit","Give them Global Administrator with a PIM approval requirement","Make them owners of a security group containing German users","Assign Privileged Authentication Administrator at tenant scope"],0,"Administrative units scope roles like Helpdesk Administrator to a subset of users. Group ownership does not grant password reset, and the other roles are tenant-wide and far too broad.","Administrative units",[null,"Global Administrator is tenant-wide and far beyond password resets; PIM approval limits when it's active, not how much it can do.","Group owners can manage membership, but ownership gives no rights to reset members' passwords.","Privileged Authentication Administrator can reset passwords for any user, including admins, across the whole tenant, which is much too broad."],null],["sc3",0,1,"You want users added to a group automatically when their department attribute is set to Finance. What do you need?",["A Microsoft 365 group with assigned membership","An access package with automatic assignment after manager approval","A distribution list synced from Exchange Online","A dynamic membership rule and Microsoft Entra ID P1 licenses"],3,"Dynamic membership rules evaluate user attributes such as department and need P1. Assigned membership is manual, and approval-based access packages are not automatic.","Create and manage groups",["Assigned membership means someone adds members by hand, so the department attribute would never add users automatically.","Access packages grant access through requests and approvals; they don't add users just because an attribute changes.","Distribution lists are Exchange mail objects and don't add members by evaluating the department attribute.",null],null],["sc4",0,1,"Which Microsoft Entra feature lets you assign Microsoft 365 licenses to users by adding them to a group?",["Administrative unit license scoping","Group-based licensing","Dynamic license provisioning in Entra Connect","Entitlement management license packages"],1,"Group-based licensing assigns licenses to all members of a group and keeps assignments as membership changes. The other options are not how licensing is assigned.","Manage licenses",["Administrative units scope admin roles to subsets of users; they don't assign licenses.",null,"Entra Connect syncs identities from on-premises AD and has no license provisioning feature.","Entitlement management access packages bundle groups, apps and sites, not license assignments as a separate object."],null],["sc5",0,1,"After a group-based license assignment, several users show an error. The service plans conflict with a license they already had directly. Where do you see and fix this?",["In the sign-in logs, filtered by the licensing service application and user","In the Licenses page for the group, which lists users in an error state","In the provisioning logs of the enterprise application","In Microsoft Entra Connect Health sync errors"],1,"The group's license view shows users with assignment errors such as conflicting service plans or not enough licenses. Sign-in and provisioning logs do not track licensing errors.","Manage licenses",["Sign-in logs record authentication attempts and don't track license assignment errors.",null,"Enterprise app provisioning logs cover SCIM provisioning to SaaS apps, not Microsoft 365 license assignment.","Connect Health reports on-premises sync and AD FS issues, not licensing conflicts in the cloud."],null],["sc6",0,1,"A company wants a small team of admins to manage a set of sensitive executive accounts, and even Global Administrators should not be able to change those accounts unless they are assigned in that scope. What should you use?",["A dynamic group with the executives and a Conditional Access policy","A PIM for Groups assignment for the executive group","A custom role with only microsoft.directory/users/password/update","A restricted management administrative unit"],3,"Restricted management administrative units block tenant-level admins from changing member objects unless they hold a role scoped to that unit. Conditional Access and custom roles do not prevent tenant-wide admins from making changes.","Administrative units",["A dynamic group plus Conditional Access controls sign-in conditions but doesn't stop tenant admins from editing those accounts.","PIM for Groups gives just-in-time group membership or ownership; it doesn't shield the members' accounts from Global Administrators.","A custom role grants its holder a permission; it doesn't take any rights away from Global Administrators.",null],null],["sc7",0,1,"You need a role that can manage users' app assignments for one enterprise app only, without broader rights. Built-in roles are too broad. What can you create?",["A custom role scoped to that application","A new administrative unit that contains the app","A Conditional Access policy targeting that app","A dynamic group that owns the app's service principal"],0,"Custom Microsoft Entra roles can be built from specific permissions and assigned at an application scope. Administrative units hold users, groups and devices, not enterprise apps.","Create custom roles",[null,"Administrative units contain users, groups and devices, not enterprise applications, so they can't scope rights to one app.","Conditional Access controls how users sign in to an app; it doesn't delegate rights to manage app assignments.","Groups can't be made owners of a service principal in this way, and ownership would give more than assignment rights."],null],["sc8",0,1,"Laptops are joined to on-premises Active Directory, and users also need Conditional Access device-based policies in the cloud. Which device identity should they have?",["Microsoft Entra registered","Microsoft Entra joined only","Microsoft Entra hybrid joined","Workplace joined in AD FS only"],2,"Hybrid joined devices stay in on-premises AD and are also registered in Microsoft Entra ID, so they can satisfy device-based Conditional Access. Registered is for personal devices, and Entra joined would remove the on-premises AD join.","Manage devices",["Registered is for personal (BYOD) devices; it doesn't represent a corporate device joined to on-premises AD.","Entra joined only would take the laptops out of the on-premises AD domain they need to stay in.",null,"Workplace join through AD FS is a legacy registration method and doesn't give the full hybrid device identity used by Conditional Access."],null],["sc9",0,1,"Partners from a supplier need to open documents in your SharePoint site. They should appear as guest users you can govern. Which feature fits?",["B2B direct connect","Cross-tenant synchronization","B2B collaboration","Microsoft Entra External ID for customers"],2,"B2B collaboration invites external users as guests in your directory. B2B direct connect creates no guest object and is used for Teams shared channels. Customer (CIAM) tenants are for consumer apps.","External identities",["B2B direct connect creates no guest objects in your directory and is only used for Teams shared channels.","Cross-tenant synchronization is for tenants within the same organization, not an outside supplier.",null,"External ID for customers is for consumer-facing apps in a separate customer tenant, not partners collaborating in SharePoint."],null],["sc10",0,1,"Two business units have separate Microsoft Entra tenants in the same company. They want users from tenant A to be created and kept updated as B2B users in tenant B automatically. What should you configure?",["Cross-tenant synchronization","B2B direct connect trust settings","Microsoft Entra Connect with two forests","A guest invitation bulk upload each week"],0,"Cross-tenant synchronization automatically creates, updates and removes B2B users across tenants in one organization. Bulk invitations are manual, and Entra Connect syncs on-premises AD, not tenants.","Cross-tenant access",[null,"B2B direct connect trust settings allow shared channel access but don't create or update B2B users in the other tenant.","Entra Connect syncs on-premises AD forests into one tenant; it doesn't sync users between two cloud tenants.","A weekly bulk invitation is manual, misses updates and removals, and isn't automatic."],null],["sc11",0,1,"Your tenant should accept MFA already completed by users in a trusted partner's tenant, so guests are not prompted twice. Where do you set this?",["External collaboration settings, guest user access","Authentication methods policy for external users","The partner's Conditional Access policy exclusions","Inbound trust settings in cross-tenant access settings"],3,"Inbound trust settings in cross-tenant access settings can trust MFA, compliant device and hybrid joined device claims from the partner tenant. External collaboration settings control invitations and guest permissions, not trust.","Cross-tenant access",["External collaboration settings control who can invite guests and what guests can see, not whether partner MFA is trusted.","The authentication methods policy decides which methods your users can use; it has no setting to trust another tenant's MFA claims.","The partner's policies apply in their tenant and can't change how your tenant evaluates MFA for guests.",null],null],["sc12",0,1,"Only admins and users in the Guest Inviter role should be able to invite guests. Where is this set?",["Cross-tenant access default outbound settings","External collaboration settings","Conditional Access for guests and external users","Terms of use for external users"],1,"External collaboration settings control who can invite guests and which domains are allowed or blocked. Cross-tenant access settings control B2B access with other Entra tenants, not who can send invitations.","External identities",["Default outbound settings control your users' access to other tenants, not who can send guest invitations.",null,"Conditional Access controls how guests sign in; it doesn't decide who can invite them.","Terms of use makes users accept an agreement; it has nothing to do with invitation permissions."],null],["sc13",0,1,"You are syncing a single AD forest and want the fewest on-premises dependencies, plus leaked credential detection in ID Protection. Which sign-in method should you pick?",["Pass-through authentication","Password hash synchronization","Federation with AD FS","Seamless SSO without any sync"],1,"PHS authenticates in the cloud, so it keeps working if on-premises is down, and it enables leaked-credential detection. PTA and AD FS both rely on on-premises servers for each sign-in.","Hybrid identity",["PTA sends each sign-in to on-premises agents, which adds dependencies, and on its own doesn't give leaked credential detection.",null,"AD FS needs a farm of on-premises federation and proxy servers, which is the most infrastructure of any option.","Seamless SSO is an add-on to PHS or PTA and doesn't authenticate users without synced identities."],null],["sc14",0,1,"Security requires that on-premises account lockout and logon hour restrictions apply the moment users sign in to Microsoft 365, but passwords must not be stored in the cloud in any form. Which method fits?",["Password hash synchronization","Cloud-only accounts with SSPR","Password hash sync with staged rollout","Pass-through authentication"],3,"PTA validates passwords against on-premises AD through agents at sign-in, so AD policies apply in real time and no hash is synced. PHS stores a hash of the hash in the cloud.","Hybrid identity",["PHS stores a hash of the password hash in the cloud, which breaks the requirement that no password data is stored there.","Cloud-only accounts aren't on-premises accounts, so on-premises lockout and logon hours wouldn't apply.","Staged rollout with PHS still syncs password hashes to the cloud; it only changes which users sign in that way.",null],null],["sc15",0,1,"A company acquired another firm whose AD forest has no network connection to the main forest. They want to sync both into one tenant with little infrastructure and highly available agents. What should they use?",["Microsoft Entra Cloud Sync","Microsoft Entra Connect Sync in staging mode","A second tenant and cross-tenant sync","AD FS with a claims provider trust"],0,"Cloud Sync uses lightweight agents that can be installed in disconnected forests, with multiple agents for high availability. Connect Sync needs one server with line of sight to every forest.","Hybrid identity",[null,"Staging mode is a standby Connect Sync server; it still needs line of sight to every forest and isn't a high-availability design.","Creating a second tenant goes against the goal of syncing both forests into one tenant.","AD FS handles federated sign-in, not synchronizing users from a disconnected forest."],null],["sc16",0,1,"You are moving from AD FS federation to cloud authentication and want to test with a pilot group before converting the whole domain. What should you use?",["Microsoft Entra Connect staging mode","Seamless SSO group filtering","Staged rollout","A Conditional Access report-only policy"],2,"Staged rollout moves selected groups to PHS or PTA while the domain stays federated. Connect staging mode is a standby sync server, not a way to change sign-in for pilot users.","Hybrid identity",["Connect staging mode is a standby sync server and doesn't change how pilot users sign in.","Seamless SSO has no group filtering feature for moving pilot users off federation.",null,"Report-only mode tests Conditional Access policies, not a change of authentication method."],null],["sc17",0,1,"By default, how often does Microsoft Entra Connect Sync run a delta synchronization cycle?",["Every 2 minutes","Every 12 hours","Every 30 minutes","Once per day at midnight UTC"],2,"The built-in scheduler runs a delta sync every 30 minutes by default. You can force one sooner with Start-ADSyncSyncCycle -PolicyType Delta.","Hybrid identity",["Two minutes is the interval for Cloud Sync provisioning cycles, not the default Connect Sync delta schedule.","Twelve hours is much longer than the default delta cycle of Connect Sync.",null,"Connect Sync doesn't run once a day; its scheduler runs delta syncs much more often."],null],["sc18",0,1,"Users on domain-joined PCs inside the corporate network should reach Microsoft 365 without typing their password, and you use PHS. Which feature adds this?",["Seamless single sign-on","Passwordless phone sign-in","Microsoft Entra application proxy","Cross-tenant trust for device claims"],0,"Seamless SSO signs users in automatically on domain-joined devices on the corporate network with PHS or PTA. The other features do not provide silent sign-in from domain-joined PCs.","Hybrid identity",[null,"Passwordless phone sign-in uses Authenticator, so users still interact on their phone instead of being signed in silently.","Application proxy publishes on-premises apps externally; it doesn't give silent sign-in to Microsoft 365.","Cross-tenant device trust applies to guests from other tenants, not domain-joined PCs on your own network."],null],["sc19",0,1,"Where can you monitor Microsoft Entra Connect sync errors, such as duplicate attribute conflicts, and get alerts about them?",["Identity Secure Score","Entitlement management reports","The Conditional Access insights workbook","Microsoft Entra Connect Health"],3,"Connect Health shows sync errors (for example duplicate proxyAddresses or UPN) and sends alerts about the sync service. Secure Score and workbooks do not report per-object sync errors.","Hybrid identity",["Identity Secure Score measures adoption of security settings and doesn't list per-object sync errors.","Entitlement management reports show access package assignments, not directory sync errors.","The Conditional Access insights workbook shows policy results for sign-ins, not sync errors.",null],null],["sc20",0,1,"An admin needs to create 400 test users from a spreadsheet in the portal without writing scripts. What should they use?",["Cross-tenant synchronization","Bulk create with the downloadable CSV template","A dynamic group rule","A lifecycle workflow joiner template that imports new users"],1,"Bulk operations in the admin center take a CSV template to create, invite or delete many users. Lifecycle workflows run tasks for existing users and do not import users from a file.","Create and manage users",["Cross-tenant synchronization copies users from another Entra tenant, not from a spreadsheet.",null,"A dynamic group rule adds existing users to a group; it can't create user accounts.","Lifecycle workflows run tasks for users who already exist; they don't import users from a file."],null],["sc21",0,1,"Guests should not be able to list other users and groups in your directory. Which setting controls this?",["Outbound access in cross-tenant access settings","Guest user access restrictions in external collaboration settings","The 'Users can register applications' user setting","The Directory Readers role assigned to all guests through a dynamic group"],1,"Guest user access restrictions decide how much of the directory guests can see, with the most restrictive level limiting them to their own objects. Assigning Directory Readers would give guests more access, not less.","External identities",["Outbound cross-tenant settings control your users' access to other tenants, not what guests can see in yours.",null,"That setting controls whether users can create app registrations, not guests' directory visibility.","Directory Readers lets guests read the directory, which gives them more visibility instead of less."],null],["sc22",0,2,"A 40-person company has no Microsoft Entra ID P1 licenses and wants a baseline that requires MFA registration and blocks legacy authentication. What should they enable?",["A Conditional Access baseline policy","Identity Protection user risk policy","Per-user MFA with app passwords","Security defaults"],3,"Security defaults are free and turn on MFA registration, protect admins and block legacy authentication. Conditional Access and ID Protection need P1/P2 licenses.","Security defaults",["There are no free Conditional Access baseline policies anymore; Conditional Access needs P1 licenses.","ID Protection risk policies need P2 licenses, which this company doesn't have.","Per-user MFA doesn't block legacy authentication, and app passwords actually let legacy clients bypass MFA.",null],null],["sc23",0,2,"You are creating your first Conditional Access policy that requires MFA for all users. What should you do first to avoid locking everyone out?",["Exclude two emergency access accounts and start in report-only mode","Enable security defaults at the same time as a backup","Include only the Global Administrator role for the first week","Block legacy authentication before creating any other policy"],0,"Emergency access accounts excluded from policies, plus report-only testing, prevent a tenant-wide lockout. Security defaults cannot be used together with Conditional Access.","Conditional Access",[null,"Security defaults must be turned off before Conditional Access policies can be enabled, so they can't run side by side as a backup.","Targeting only Global Administrators first risks locking out the very admins who would need to fix the policy.","Blocking legacy authentication is a good policy, but it doesn't protect against lockout from an MFA policy."],null],["sc24",0,2,"Admins must sign in only with phishing-resistant methods such as passkeys or Windows Hello for Business. Which Conditional Access grant control enforces this?",["Require multifactor authentication","Require approved client app","Require authentication strength","Require password change"],2,"Authentication strength lets you require specific methods, such as the built-in phishing-resistant MFA strength. Plain 'Require MFA' also accepts weaker methods such as SMS.","Conditional Access",["Plain Require MFA accepts any registered second factor, including SMS and voice, which aren't phishing-resistant.","Require approved client app checks which client app is used, not which authentication method the admin used.",null,"Require password change is used with user risk policies and doesn't enforce any specific sign-in method."],null],["sc25",0,2,"A new hire has no password and no MFA method yet but must register a passkey on day one. What should the admin issue?",["A one-time SMS code from SSPR","A client secret on an app registration","A Temporary Access Pass","An app password"],2,"A Temporary Access Pass is a time-limited code that lets a user sign in and register strong methods. SSPR needs an already registered method, and app passwords are for legacy clients.","Authentication methods",["SSPR can only send codes to a method the user already registered, and this new hire has none.","Client secrets are credentials for applications, not for users signing in.",null,"App passwords are for legacy clients that can't do MFA and can't be used to register a passkey."],null],["sc26",0,2,"You want users who still rely on SMS to be prompted to set up Microsoft Authenticator when they sign in, with a limited number of snoozes. What should you configure?",["A registration campaign","System-preferred MFA","A sign-in risk policy","Combined registration interrupt in SSPR"],0,"A registration campaign nudges users at sign-in to register Authenticator and can limit snoozes. System-preferred MFA picks which already registered method is offered first, but does not ask users to register a new one.","Authentication methods",[null,"System-preferred MFA picks the strongest already registered method at sign-in; it doesn't ask users to register a new one.","A sign-in risk policy reacts to risky sign-ins and doesn't nudge users to set up Authenticator.","Combined registration interrupts ask for missing required methods but don't target SMS users with a limited-snooze Authenticator prompt."],null],["sc27",0,2,"Users with several registered methods should be prompted with the most secure one they have instead of the one they last used. Which feature does this?",["Authentication method registration campaign","Number matching","Sign-in frequency session control","System-preferred multifactor authentication"],3,"System-preferred MFA picks the strongest registered method at sign-in. Number matching is a feature of Authenticator push, not method selection.","Authentication methods",["A registration campaign prompts users to register Authenticator; it doesn't choose which registered method is offered at sign-in.","Number matching makes users type a number in Authenticator push notifications; it doesn't choose between methods.","Sign-in frequency controls how often users reauthenticate, not which method they are prompted for.",null],null],["sc28",0,2,"Hybrid users reset their password through SSPR, but they still cannot sign in to on-premises resources with the new password. What is missing?",["Seamless single sign-on","Password writeback in Entra Connect or Cloud Sync","A custom banned password list","Microsoft Entra hybrid join for every Windows device"],1,"Password writeback sends cloud password changes back to on-premises AD. Without it, the reset only changes the cloud password.","SSPR",["Seamless SSO provides silent sign-in on domain-joined PCs and doesn't send new passwords to on-premises AD.",null,"A custom banned password list blocks weak passwords but doesn't sync password changes to on-premises AD.","Hybrid join is a device identity feature and doesn't write reset passwords back to AD."],null],["sc29",0,2,"The company wants to stop users from choosing passwords that contain the company name or product names. What should you configure?",["Smart lockout threshold and duration","A custom banned password list in Password Protection","A Conditional Access user risk policy","Password expiration of 30 days in the tenant's password policy settings"],1,"The custom banned password list (up to 1,000 terms) blocks those words and variations. Smart lockout protects against guessing, not weak password choices.","Password protection",["Smart lockout limits password guessing attempts but doesn't stop users from choosing weak passwords.",null,"A user risk policy responds to compromised accounts and doesn't check the words in a new password.","Frequent expiration makes users rotate passwords more often but doesn't block company or product names."],null],["sc30",0,2,"You want the banned password lists to apply when users change passwords on on-premises domain controllers too. What must you deploy?",["The Microsoft Entra Connect Health agent for AD DS","Pass-through authentication agents","The application proxy private network connector","The Password Protection DC agent and proxy service"],3,"Password Protection for AD DS needs the DC agent on domain controllers and the proxy service that fetches policy from the cloud. PTA agents validate sign-ins, not password changes.","Password protection",["The Connect Health AD DS agent monitors domain controllers but doesn't enforce banned password lists.","PTA agents validate cloud sign-ins against AD; they don't check passwords when users change them.","The private network connector publishes internal apps and has nothing to do with password policy.",null],null],["sc31",0,2,"Attackers are trying many passwords against accounts, and legitimate users keep getting locked out too. Which feature locks out attackers while trying to let real users keep working?",["Smart lockout","A custom banned password list","Temporary Access Pass","Continuous access evaluation"],0,"Smart lockout uses familiar vs unfamiliar location data to lock out attackers while trying to keep real users working, with a configurable threshold and duration.","Password protection",[null,"A banned password list stops weak password choices but doesn't respond to repeated failed attempts.","A Temporary Access Pass is a time-limited sign-in code for onboarding or recovery, not a defense against guessing.","CAE revokes sessions after events like account disable; it doesn't lock out repeated failed password attempts."],null],["sc32",0,2,"Sign-ins from countries where the company does not operate must be blocked. What do you create before the Conditional Access policy?",["A dynamic device group with a region rule","A Global Secure Access remote network","A named location with those countries","A terms of use document per country"],2,"Named locations define IP ranges or countries that Conditional Access can include or exclude. Device groups and terms of use do not represent network location.","Conditional Access",["Device groups group device objects by attributes and can't represent where a sign-in is coming from.","A Global Secure Access remote network connects branch offices to the service; it doesn't define countries for Conditional Access.",null,"Terms of use requires accepting an agreement and doesn't identify or block countries."],null],["sc33",0,2,"A policy should apply only to devices whose model attribute is a specific kiosk model. Which Conditional Access condition supports this?",["Device platforms","Client apps","Filter for devices","Insider risk"],2,"Filter for devices uses device attributes such as model, deviceId or extensionAttributes. Device platforms only matches the OS type.","Conditional Access",["Device platforms only match the operating system type, such as Windows or iOS, not the device model.","Client apps matches browser, mobile/desktop or legacy clients, not device attributes.",null,"Insider risk is based on user behavior signals from Purview, not a device's model attribute."],null],["sc34",0,2,"Users on unmanaged devices should use SharePoint in the browser but be unable to download files. Which Conditional Access control helps?",["Session control: use app enforced restrictions or Conditional Access App Control","Grant control: require approved client app","Grant control: require terms of use","Session control: sign-in frequency every hour for unmanaged devices in SharePoint"],0,"App enforced restrictions (in SharePoint/Exchange) or Conditional Access App Control through Defender for Cloud Apps can allow browser access and block downloads. Sign-in frequency only changes how often users must sign in again.","Conditional Access",[null,"Require approved client app forces specific mobile apps; it doesn't allow browser access while blocking downloads.","Terms of use makes users accept an agreement and doesn't restrict downloads.","Sign-in frequency only changes how often users reauthenticate; downloads would still be allowed."],null],["sc35",0,2,"Admins must sign in again every 4 hours on the Azure portal, even with a valid session. What should you configure?",["Persistent browser session set to never","Continuous access evaluation strict mode","Token lifetime policy for access tokens","Sign-in frequency session control"],3,"Sign-in frequency sets how long before users must reauthenticate. Persistent browser session controls whether the browser session survives closing the browser.","Conditional Access",["Persistent browser session controls whether the session survives closing the browser, not a fixed reauthentication interval.","CAE strict mode enforces location and critical events; it doesn't make admins reauthenticate on a schedule.","Configurable token lifetimes for access tokens don't force a fresh interactive sign-in every 4 hours, and refresh tokens would keep the session alive.",null],null],["sc36",0,2,"A user is unexpectedly blocked from an app. You want to know which Conditional Access policies would apply for that user, app, location and device without them signing in again. Which tool do you use?",["Report-only mode","The What If tool","Identity Secure Score","Access reviews"],1,"The What If tool simulates a sign-in with the conditions you choose and lists which policies would apply. Report-only mode logs results only when real sign-ins happen.","Conditional Access",["Report-only mode records results only when real sign-ins happen, so you'd have to wait for the user to sign in again.",null,"Identity Secure Score measures overall security posture and doesn't evaluate policies for one user's sign-in.","Access reviews confirm whether users still need access; they don't show which Conditional Access policies apply."],null],["sc37",0,2,"Which risk type is most closely tied to leaked credentials found by Microsoft?",["Sign-in risk","User risk","Service principal risk","Device risk"],1,"Leaked credentials is a user risk detection, because it says the account itself may be compromised, not one sign-in.","ID Protection",["Sign-in risk is about one specific sign-in looking suspicious, while leaked credentials concern the account itself.",null,"Service principal risk applies to workload identities, not leaked credentials of user accounts.","Device risk comes from endpoint signals like Defender for Endpoint, not from leaked credential detections."],null],["sc38",0,2,"When sign-in risk is medium or high, users should be able to continue after proving their identity. Which policy setup is recommended?",["Conditional Access with user risk condition and block access","Security defaults with risk-based prompts turned on","A named location that excludes risky countries","Conditional Access with sign-in risk condition and require MFA"],3,"A risk-based Conditional Access policy with sign-in risk and MFA lets users self-remediate. Blocking would stop the user completely, and security defaults cannot be customized.","ID Protection",["This uses user risk instead of sign-in risk, and blocking access doesn't let users continue after proving their identity.","Security defaults can't be customized and have no configurable risk-based prompts.","Named locations filter by IP or country; excluding countries doesn't evaluate sign-in risk.",null],null],["sc39",0,2,"A user's risk is high because of leaked credentials. The recommended Conditional Access policy should let the user fix this themselves. What grant should it require?",["Require risk remediation or a secure password change","Require compliant device","Require terms of use acceptance with a new expiry date","Require approved client app"],0,"For user risk, Microsoft recommends requiring a secure password change (or risk remediation) so the risk is cleared. Device compliance does not fix a compromised credential.","ID Protection",[null,"A compliant device doesn't fix a compromised password, so the user risk would remain.","Accepting terms of use doesn't change the leaked password or clear user risk.","Approved client apps control which app is used; they don't remediate a compromised credential."],null],["sc40",0,2,"A user is disabled after they leave, but their access token for Exchange Online is still valid for another 45 minutes. Which feature lets Exchange reject the session quickly?",["Sign-in frequency","Persistent browser session","Continuous access evaluation","Smart lockout"],2,"CAE lets supported services like Exchange Online and SharePoint react in near real time to critical events such as account disable. Sign-in frequency only affects the next authentication.","Conditional Access",["Sign-in frequency only takes effect at the next authentication, so the existing token keeps working until then.","Persistent browser session only controls whether browser sessions survive closing the browser.",null,"Smart lockout blocks repeated failed password attempts; it doesn't revoke existing sessions."],null],["sc41",0,2,"Your company wants to replace its legacy VPN with identity-aware access to internal apps and file shares. Which Microsoft Entra service fits?",["Microsoft Entra Internet Access","Microsoft Entra ID Protection","Microsoft Entra Private Access","Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps discovery"],2,"Private Access, part of Global Secure Access, gives Zero Trust access to private apps and can replace VPNs. Internet Access secures traffic to internet and SaaS destinations.","Global Secure Access",["Internet Access secures traffic to internet and SaaS destinations, not internal apps and file shares.","ID Protection detects identity risk; it doesn't give network access to private apps.",null,"Cloud discovery finds which SaaS apps people use from traffic logs; it doesn't provide access to internal resources."],null],["sc42",0,2,"Global Secure Access should secure only Microsoft 365 traffic at first. What do you turn on?",["The Microsoft traffic forwarding profile","The private access forwarding profile","A named location for Microsoft 365","A Conditional Access policy for the Office 365 app"],0,"Traffic forwarding profiles choose which traffic the Global Secure Access client sends: Microsoft, Internet access or Private access. Named locations do not forward traffic.","Global Secure Access",[null,"The Private Access profile forwards traffic to internal apps, not Microsoft 365.","Named locations describe IP ranges or countries for Conditional Access; they don't forward any traffic.","A Conditional Access policy controls sign-in to Office 365 but doesn't send traffic through Global Secure Access."],null],["sc43",0,2,"Which statement about security defaults and Conditional Access is correct?",["Security defaults apply first, then Conditional Access adds rules","Conditional Access only works when security defaults is enabled","Security defaults can be scoped to exclude named groups","They cannot both be enabled; you must disable security defaults first"],3,"Security defaults must be turned off before you enable Conditional Access policies. Security defaults cannot be scoped or exclude groups.","Security defaults",["They don't layer this way; security defaults have to be disabled before Conditional Access policies can be turned on.","It's the reverse: Conditional Access requires security defaults to be turned off.","Security defaults are all-or-nothing and can't exclude groups or be scoped.",null],null],["sc44",0,2,"Shared kiosk users need to sign in without passwords using a smart card with an X.509 certificate issued by your internal CA. What must you configure?",["Temporary Access Pass for each kiosk user","Certificate-based authentication with the CA uploaded to the tenant","SMS sign-in in the authentication methods policy","Windows Hello for Business cloud Kerberos trust on every shared kiosk device"],1,"Microsoft Entra certificate-based authentication validates user certificates once you upload the issuing CA and enable the method. TAP is temporary, and Windows Hello is tied to one device.","Authentication methods",["A TAP is a temporary code for onboarding or recovery and doesn't use smart card certificates.",null,"SMS sign-in uses a phone number and text message, not an X.509 certificate on a smart card.","Windows Hello for Business is tied to one user on one device, which doesn't suit shared kiosks using smart cards."],null],["sc45",0,2,"Which grant control can you add to Conditional Access to make sure a device is marked compliant by Intune before users access an app?",["Require Microsoft Entra hybrid joined device","Require device to be marked as compliant","Require approved client app","Require authentication strength"],1,"'Require device to be marked as compliant' checks Intune compliance. Hybrid joined only checks join state, not compliance.","Conditional Access",["Hybrid joined only checks that the device is joined to on-premises AD and registered in Entra, not that Intune marks it compliant.",null,"Require approved client app checks the client app used, not device compliance.","Authentication strength controls which sign-in methods count, not the device's compliance state."],null],["sc46",0,2,"An organization wants refresh tokens to be bound to the device they were issued to, so stolen tokens cannot be replayed elsewhere. Which session control addresses this?",["Sign-in frequency","Persistent browser session","Use app enforced restrictions","Token protection"],3,"Token protection binds sign-in session tokens to the device, reducing token theft and replay. The other controls set timing or app behavior.","Conditional Access",["Sign-in frequency sets how often users reauthenticate but doesn't bind tokens to a device.","Persistent browser session controls whether sessions survive closing the browser; stolen tokens could still be replayed.","App enforced restrictions let SharePoint and Exchange limit features such as downloads, not bind tokens to a device.",null],null],["sc47",0,3,"An Azure Function needs to read secrets from Key Vault without any stored credentials. The permission should be deleted with the Function. What should you use?",["A system-assigned managed identity","A user-assigned managed identity","An app registration with a client secret","A service account user with a long password"],0,"A system-assigned managed identity is tied to one resource and deleted with it. A user-assigned identity survives separately, and secrets or service accounts need credential handling.","Managed identities",[null,"A user-assigned identity is a separate resource with its own lifecycle, so it wouldn't be deleted with the Function.","A client secret is a stored credential that has to be protected and rotated, which the requirement rules out.","A service account password is a stored credential and isn't deleted when the Function is removed."],null],["sc48",0,3,"Twenty VMs in a scale set and three App Services all need the same access to a storage account. You want one identity to manage and it should outlive any single resource. What fits best?",["One system-assigned identity per resource","A shared guest account","A user-assigned managed identity","An app registration with a certificate on each VM"],2,"A user-assigned managed identity is a standalone resource that can be attached to many resources and keeps its own lifecycle. System-assigned would mean 23 identities to manage.","Managed identities",["A system-assigned identity per resource means 23 separate identities, each deleted with its own resource.","A shared guest account is a user identity with a password and isn't meant for Azure resources to authenticate.",null,"Certificates on each VM have to be distributed and rotated, and they don't cover the App Services neatly."],null],["sc49",0,3,"A background service with no signed-in user must read all users through Microsoft Graph. Which permission type and consent are needed?",["Delegated permission with user consent","Delegated permission with admin consent","Application permission with admin consent","Application permission with user consent"],2,"Daemon apps use application permissions, which always need admin consent. Delegated permissions need a signed-in user, and users cannot consent to application permissions.","App permissions",["Delegated permissions act on behalf of a signed-in user, and there's no user in a background service.","Delegated permissions still require a signed-in user even when an admin has consented to them.",null,"Users can never consent to application permissions; only an admin can."],null],["sc50",0,3,"Users should be able to consent only to apps from verified publishers asking for low-risk permissions. All other requests should go to reviewers. What do you configure?",["User consent settings plus the admin consent workflow","Assignment required on every enterprise app","A Conditional Access policy for all cloud apps","App roles in each application manifest assigned to all users by default"],0,"User consent settings can allow verified publishers with selected permissions, and the admin consent workflow routes other requests to reviewers. 'Assignment required' controls who can sign in, not consent.","Consent",[null,"Assignment required controls who can sign in to an app, not who can consent to its permissions.","Conditional Access controls how users sign in and has no setting for app consent.","App roles define authorization inside an app; they don't control consent to permissions."],null],["sc51",0,3,"Only members of the HR group should be able to sign in to an enterprise app. What should you do?",["Hide the app from My Apps for other users","Remove the delegated permissions from the app","Create an app role called HR in the manifest","Set Assignment required to Yes and assign the HR group"],3,"With Assignment required on, only assigned users and groups can get tokens for the app. Hiding it in My Apps does not block sign-in, and an app role alone does not restrict access.","Enterprise applications",["Hiding the app from My Apps only removes the tile; users could still sign in with a direct link.","Delegated permissions control what the app can access, not which users can sign in to it.","Defining an app role alone doesn't restrict sign-in unless assignment is required and users are assigned.",null],null],["sc52",0,3,"A developer wants the app to receive a claim showing whether the user is a Reader or an Approver. What should be defined and assigned?",["Directory roles such as Application Administrator","App roles, which appear in the roles claim","Custom security attributes on the app","Optional claims for group names"],1,"App roles are defined on the app registration and assigned to users or groups, and they appear in the token's roles claim. Directory roles are for managing Entra ID, not app authorization.","App roles",["Directory roles such as Application Administrator grant rights to manage Entra ID, not authorization inside the developer's app.",null,"Custom security attributes describe the app for governance and don't appear as a user's role in the token.","Group name claims send group membership, not app-specific roles like Reader or Approver that are defined on the app."],null],["sc53",0,3,"You are setting up SAML SSO for a gallery app. The vendor gives you an Entity ID and an Assertion Consumer Service URL. Where do these go?",["Redirect URI and Application ID URI in app registration","Identifier (Entity ID) and Reply URL in Basic SAML Configuration","Home page URL and Logout URL in Properties","Named location and client app condition in Conditional Access"],1,"The Identifier and Reply URL (ACS) fields in Basic SAML Configuration match what the service provider expects. Redirect URIs are for OAuth/OIDC apps.","SSO",["Redirect URI and Application ID URI belong to OAuth/OIDC app registrations, not SAML configuration of a gallery app.",null,"Home page and logout URLs are app properties for navigation; they don't set the SAML Entity ID or ACS.","Conditional Access settings control sign-in conditions and have nowhere to enter SAML values."],null],["sc54",0,3,"A SAML app stopped working for everyone this morning. The sign-in logs show an error about the signature. What is the most likely cause?",["The Assignment required setting was turned off","A user's license was removed","Seamless SSO was disabled on the domain","The SAML signing certificate expired or was changed"],3,"If the Entra signing certificate expires or is rotated without updating the service provider, assertions fail signature checks for everyone. Turning off assignment would allow more users, not break SSO.","SSO",["Turning off assignment required would let more users sign in; it wouldn't cause signature errors.","Removing one user's license wouldn't break SSO for everyone, and it doesn't affect SAML signatures.","Seamless SSO covers silent sign-in on domain-joined PCs; disabling it doesn't cause SAML signature errors.",null],null],["sc55",0,3,"New hires should get accounts in a SaaS app automatically, and leavers should be disabled there. The app supports SCIM 2.0. What do you configure?",["Automatic user provisioning on the enterprise app","A lifecycle workflow with a custom Logic App only","Cross-tenant synchronization to the vendor tenant","B2B direct connect with the SaaS vendor"],0,"Automatic provisioning uses SCIM to create, update and disable users in the app based on assignments and scoping filters. Cross-tenant sync is only between Entra tenants.","Provisioning",[null,"A lifecycle workflow with only a custom Logic App would be custom work and ignores the app's built-in SCIM support.","Cross-tenant synchronization only works between Microsoft Entra tenants, not a SaaS app's own user store.","B2B direct connect is for Teams shared channels between Entra tenants and doesn't create SaaS accounts."],null],["sc56",0,3,"Where do you check why a specific user was not created in a SaaS app by automatic provisioning?",["The audit logs of Microsoft Entra Connect","The sign-in logs for the app","The provisioning logs","Identity Secure Score"],2,"Provisioning logs show each create, update and skip action with the reason, for example an out-of-scope user. Sign-in logs record authentications, not provisioning.","Provisioning",["Entra Connect audit data covers on-premises sync, not provisioning from Entra ID to SaaS apps.","Sign-in logs record authentications, not whether an account was created in the app.",null,"Identity Secure Score measures security posture and doesn't show provisioning actions."],null],["sc57",0,3,"An internal web app on an on-premises server must be reachable by remote users with Microsoft Entra sign-in and MFA, without opening inbound firewall ports. What should you use?",["A site-to-site VPN and a public IP address on the server","Password hash synchronization with Seamless SSO","Microsoft Entra application proxy with a private network connector","B2B direct connect with the on-premises domain"],2,"Application proxy connectors make outbound connections only, and users authenticate with Entra ID and Conditional Access. A public IP would need inbound ports.","Application proxy",["A public IP address needs inbound firewall ports, which the requirement rules out.","PHS with Seamless SSO handles authentication but doesn't publish an internal web app to remote users.",null,"B2B direct connect is for Teams shared channels between tenants and doesn't publish on-premises apps."],null],["sc58",0,3,"You want to find which unsanctioned cloud apps employees are using, based on firewall logs. Which feature helps?",["Cloud discovery in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps","Access reviews for enterprise apps","Microsoft Entra Connect Health with the AD FS agent on each proxy","Admin consent requests"],0,"Cloud discovery analyzes traffic logs to find shadow IT apps and give risk scores. Access reviews only cover apps already integrated with Entra ID.","Defender for Cloud Apps",[null,"Access reviews only cover apps already integrated with Entra ID, not unsanctioned apps found in traffic.","The AD FS agent monitors federation servers and doesn't analyze firewall logs for app usage.","Admin consent requests only show apps users tried to consent to, not all SaaS traffic from firewall logs."],null],["sc59",0,3,"A single-tenant service principal used by a CI pipeline should only be able to get tokens from your build agents' public IP range. What can enforce this?",["A named location assigned to the app registration","Assignment required on the service principal","A user risk policy targeting service principals","Conditional Access for workload identities with a location condition"],3,"Conditional Access for workload identities (Workload Identities Premium) can block service principals outside given locations. Named locations alone do nothing unless used in a policy.","Workload identities",["Named locations can't be assigned to app registrations directly; they only take effect inside a Conditional Access policy.","Assignment required controls which users can sign in to an app, not where a service principal gets tokens from.","User risk policies apply to users, not service principals, and don't restrict by IP range.",null],null],["sc60",0,3,"A GitHub Actions workflow deploys to Azure. Security wants no long-lived secrets stored in GitHub. What should the app registration use?",["A client secret with a 24-month expiry","A federated identity credential for GitHub","A certificate stored as a GitHub secret","A user account with MFA excluded"],1,"Workload identity federation trusts tokens from GitHub's OIDC issuer, so no secret is stored. Certificates and secrets are still long-lived credentials that can leak.","Workload identities",["A client secret is exactly the kind of long-lived secret stored in GitHub that security wants to avoid.",null,"A certificate stored as a GitHub secret is still a long-lived credential that could leak.","A user account with MFA excluded is insecure and still needs a stored password."],null],["sc61",0,3,"Which statement about application objects and service principals is correct?",["Each tenant using a multitenant app gets its own copy of the application object","The app object is the global definition; a service principal is its local instance in each tenant","Service principals exist only for managed identities, not for apps","Deleting a service principal also deletes the app object in the home tenant"],1,"The application object lives in the home tenant; each tenant that uses the app gets a service principal. Deleting a service principal does not remove the home app object.","Workload identities",["It's the reverse: other tenants get a service principal, while the single application object stays in the home tenant.",null,"Every app used in a tenant has a service principal, not just managed identities.","Deleting a service principal removes only that tenant's local instance; the app object in the home tenant remains."],null],["sc62",0,3,"Security wants to find app registrations whose client secrets expire within 30 days, and apps that have not signed in for months. Where should you start?",["Entitlement management catalog reports","The Conditional Access insights and reporting workbook for applications","Password Protection audit logs on DCs","Microsoft Entra recommendations and the app registrations credentials view"],3,"Entra recommendations flag expiring credentials and unused apps, and the app registrations list shows certificates and secrets with expiry dates. The other tools do not track app credentials.","Workload identities",["Entitlement management reports cover access package assignments, not app credentials or app sign-in activity.","The Conditional Access insights workbook shows policy results, not credential expiry dates.","Password Protection logs on DCs record password changes checked against banned lists, not app registrations.",null],null],["sc63",0,3,"Users on unmanaged devices should be able to open Salesforce, but downloads of files labeled Confidential must be blocked in real time. What should you configure?",["A Conditional Access App Control session policy in Defender for Cloud Apps","An app role that removes download rights","An access review of Salesforce users","The Salesforce provisioning scoping filter based on the Confidential label"],0,"Routing the session through Conditional Access App Control lets Defender for Cloud Apps session policies block downloads live. App roles and provisioning do not inspect sessions.","Defender for Cloud Apps",[null,"App roles are claims the app interprets; they can't block downloads of labeled files in real time.","An access review confirms who needs access; it doesn't control downloads during a session.","Provisioning scoping filters decide which users get accounts, and they don't inspect file labels."],null],["sc64",0,3,"A new app needs Mail.Send delegated permission. Users see 'Need admin approval' and cannot continue. What is the best way to let them request access?",["Grant Global Administrator to the requesting users","Turn on 'Users can register applications'","Enable the admin consent workflow and name reviewers","Set the app to multitenant"],2,"The admin consent workflow lets users submit a request that designated reviewers approve or deny. Granting admin roles to users breaks least privilege.","Consent",["Making users Global Administrators breaks least privilege badly just to grant one app permission.","That setting lets users create app registrations; it doesn't let them get admin consent for this app.",null,"Making the app multitenant changes who can use it, not how admin-approved permissions are requested."],null],["sc65",0,3,"ID Protection shows a service principal with high risk because its credentials appeared in a public code repository. What should you do first?",["Add the service principal to an access review","Exclude it from all Conditional Access policies","Remove the leaked credential and add a new one","Change its owner to another user"],2,"A leaked credential should be removed and replaced right away, then you investigate activity. An access review or new owner would not stop use of the leaked secret.","Workload identities",["An access review is slow and doesn't stop anyone from using the leaked credential right now.","Excluding it from Conditional Access would reduce protection for a compromised identity.",null,"Changing the owner doesn't invalidate the leaked credential, which still works."],null],["sc66",0,3,"A web app signs users in and calls an API on their behalf. It is built on OpenID Connect. Which kind of SSO do you configure for it in Microsoft Entra ID?",["OIDC/OAuth through the app registration","Password-based SSO in My Apps","Linked SSO to an external URL","Header-based SSO through a SAML proxy"],0,"Modern apps that use OpenID Connect are configured through app registration with redirect URIs and permissions. Password-based SSO stores credentials for legacy apps.","SSO",[null,"Password-based SSO stores and replays credentials for legacy apps that can't use modern protocols.","Linked SSO only adds a link in My Apps and doesn't sign the user in with OIDC.","Header-based SSO is for legacy on-premises apps that read identity from HTTP headers, not OIDC apps."],null],["sc67",0,3,"The finance team wants a list of which users signed in to each enterprise app in the last 30 days, to spot unused apps. Where do you look?",["Provisioning logs","Audit logs filtered by the consent and app role assignment categories","Entitlement management access package reports","Sign-in logs, or the application usage workbook"],3,"Sign-in logs (and workbooks built on them) show app sign-in activity. Audit logs record changes, not sign-ins.","Monitoring apps",["Provisioning logs show account create and update actions in apps, not user sign-ins.","Audit logs record configuration changes like consents and role assignments, not sign-in activity.","Access package reports show who was assigned packages, not who actually signed in to each app.",null],null],["sc68",0,4,"Contractors should get a bundle of a Teams team, a SharePoint site and an app for 90 days after their manager approves. Which feature fits?",["A dynamic group with an expiry date","An access package in entitlement management","A PIM for Groups eligible assignment","A lifecycle workflow mover template"],1,"Access packages bundle resources with request, approval and expiration policies. Dynamic groups have no approval or expiry.","Entitlement management",["Dynamic groups add members based on attributes and have no manager approval or built-in expiry.",null,"PIM for Groups gives just-in-time group membership, not a request-and-approval bundle of a team, site and app for contractors.","A mover workflow reacts to attribute changes for existing employees; it doesn't handle approved requests for contractors."],null],["sc69",0,4,"Users from a partner organization with their own Microsoft Entra tenant should be able to request an access package themselves. What must you set up?",["A B2B direct connect trust with the partner","A connected organization for the partner","Cross-tenant synchronization from the partner","An administrative unit for partner users"],1,"Connected organizations let users in that external directory or domain request access packages, and they are added as guests on approval. Cross-tenant sync is for tenants in the same organization.","Entitlement management",["B2B direct connect is for Teams shared channels and doesn't let partners request access packages.",null,"Cross-tenant synchronization is for tenants in the same organization and pushes users rather than letting partners request access.","Administrative units scope admin roles and have nothing to do with external users requesting access."],null],["sc70",0,4,"Users who have the Payment Approver access package must not also get the Payment Creator package. What should you configure?",["A Conditional Access block policy","Different catalogs for the two packages","Access review auto-apply with deny","Incompatible access packages (separation of duties)"],3,"Entitlement management separation of duties lets you mark access packages or groups as incompatible so a user cannot request both. Separate catalogs do not prevent that.","Entitlement management",["Conditional Access controls sign-in conditions; it can't prevent a user from being assigned a second access package.","Catalogs organize resources and delegation; a user could still request packages from both catalogs.","An access review removes access periodically after the fact; it doesn't stop the second package being assigned.",null],null],["sc71",0,4,"Each quarter, group owners must confirm whether guest members still need access, and access should be removed if an owner does not respond. What do you create?",["A recurring access review with auto-apply and 'remove access' if no response","A PIM alert for stale assignments","A lifecycle workflow leaver template","A Conditional Access policy for guests that blocks sign-ins after 90 days of inactivity"],0,"Recurring access reviews can target guests in groups, use owners as reviewers, and auto-apply removal when reviewers don't respond. PIM alerts concern role assignments.","Access reviews",[null,"PIM alerts cover privileged role settings and assignments, not guest membership in groups.","A leaver workflow runs when an employee leaves; it doesn't ask owners to confirm guests every quarter.","Conditional Access can't block sign-ins based on 90 days of inactivity, and it doesn't involve owners or remove membership."],null],["sc72",0,4,"Reviewers want help deciding in an access review. Which built-in recommendation is based on activity?",["Approve all users who have a manager attribute that matches the group owner","Deny for users without a license","Deny for users who have not signed in within the inactivity period","Approve users who registered MFA"],2,"Access reviews recommend denying users who have been inactive (no sign-in) for the set period, and can also use user-to-group affiliation. License and MFA state are not recommendation inputs.","Access reviews",["Manager-to-owner matching isn't a recommendation input; the affiliation recommendation compares users to the group's other members.","License state isn't used to generate access review recommendations.",null,"MFA registration isn't a recommendation input and says nothing about whether the user still needs access."],null],["sc73",0,4,"The security team wants helpdesk staff to hold User Administrator only when needed, for up to 2 hours, with MFA and a justification. What should you use?",["A permanent active assignment scoped to an administrative unit","A custom role with fewer permissions","An eligible assignment in PIM with activation settings","An access package that includes the role for 2 hours"],2,"PIM eligible assignments require activation, and role settings set maximum duration, MFA and justification. Active assignments give standing access.","PIM",["A permanent active assignment is standing access, even when scoped to an administrative unit.","A custom role reduces permissions but still gives standing access with no activation, time limit or justification.",null,"Access packages grant assignments through requests and approvals; they don't give eligible roles with on-demand 2-hour activation, MFA and justification the way PIM does."],null],["sc74",0,4,"Activating Global Administrator in PIM must be approved by two security leads. Where is this set?",["The role settings for Global Administrator in PIM","The Conditional Access policy for Directory roles","The access package policy for admin roles","The administrative unit that contains the admins"],0,"PIM role settings define approval and approvers for activation. Conditional Access controls sign-in, not role activation approval.","PIM",[null,"Conditional Access controls sign-in conditions and doesn't approve PIM role activations.","Access package policies govern access package requests, not PIM activation of Global Administrator.","Administrative units scope roles to sets of objects and have no activation approval settings."],null],["sc75",0,4,"Admins need just-in-time membership in a security group that grants access to sensitive Azure resources. Which feature covers this?",["A dynamic group with an admin rule","Access reviews of the group","Group-based licensing","PIM for Groups"],3,"PIM for Groups allows eligible membership or ownership with time-limited activation. Dynamic groups and access reviews do not give just-in-time membership.","PIM",["A dynamic group adds members from attributes and gives permanent membership, not time-limited activation.","Access reviews check existing membership periodically; they don't give just-in-time membership.","Group-based licensing assigns licenses to members and doesn't grant just-in-time membership.",null],null],["sc76",0,4,"A PIM alert says there are too many Global Administrators. What is the recommended fix?",["Delete the alert rule because Global Administrators are required","Convert most to eligible or lower roles, keeping a small number plus break-glass accounts","Add all Global Administrators to an administrative unit","Exclude them from MFA and require compliant devices to reduce support calls and lockouts"],1,"Microsoft recommends fewer than five Global Administrators, using least-privileged roles and eligible assignments. Administrative units do not scope Global Administrator.","PIM",["Deleting the alert hides the problem instead of reducing standing Global Administrator access.",null,"Administrative units can't scope Global Administrator, which is always tenant-wide.","Excluding admins from MFA would make the riskiest accounts much weaker."],null],["sc77",0,4,"New hires should receive a Temporary Access Pass and group memberships automatically before their first day, based on the employeeHireDate attribute. What should you use?",["An access review scoped to new users","A lifecycle workflow using the onboarding template","A dynamic group with a hire-date rule and SSPR","A PIM eligible assignment that starts on hire date"],1,"Lifecycle workflows run joiner tasks, such as generating a TAP and adding groups, triggered by employeeHireDate. Dynamic groups cannot issue a TAP.","Lifecycle workflows",["Access reviews confirm existing access periodically and can't issue a TAP before day one.",null,"Dynamic groups can add memberships but can't generate a Temporary Access Pass, and SSPR needs registered methods.","PIM eligible assignments cover privileged roles and groups, not TAP generation and onboarding tasks."],null],["sc78",0,4,"Which license is needed for lifecycle workflows?",["Microsoft Entra ID P1 only","Microsoft Entra ID Free","Workload Identities Premium","Microsoft Entra ID Governance"],3,"Lifecycle workflows are a Microsoft Entra ID Governance feature. P1 covers features such as Conditional Access and dynamic groups.","Licensing",["P1 includes features such as Conditional Access and dynamic groups, but not lifecycle workflows.","The Free tier doesn't include any identity governance automation like lifecycle workflows.","Workload Identities Premium covers features for service principals, not user lifecycle workflows.",null],null],["sc79",0,4,"Guests must accept a data-handling agreement before they can open any company app, and accept it again every year. What should you use?",["Terms of use with a Conditional Access grant control","A custom banner in company branding","An access package with a questionnaire","An access review that guests complete themselves"],0,"Terms of use can be required through Conditional Access and set to expire so users accept again. Branding only shows text and does not enforce acceptance.","Terms of use",[null,"Company branding only changes the sign-in page look and text; it can't force acceptance or re-acceptance.","An access package questionnaire is answered when requesting a package, not before opening every app each year.","Access reviews confirm whether access is still needed; they don't make users accept an agreement."],null],["sc80",0,4,"Your tenant has P1 licenses. An investigation needs sign-in logs from 60 days ago, but they are gone. What should have been in place?",["A longer smart lockout duration","Identity Secure Score history export","A diagnostic setting sending logs to Log Analytics or storage","Security defaults with extended audit mode and a 90-day log retention setting"],2,"With P1/P2, sign-in logs stay 30 days in Entra ID. Diagnostic settings send them to Log Analytics, storage or Event Hubs for longer retention.","Monitoring",["Smart lockout duration controls how long accounts stay locked after failed attempts, not log retention.","Secure Score history shows posture over time, not individual sign-in log entries.",null,"Security defaults have no extended audit mode or retention setting."],null],["sc81",0,4,"How long are sign-in and audit logs kept in a tenant with only Microsoft Entra ID Free?",["30 days","90 days","7 days","1 year"],2,"Free tenants keep these logs for 7 days, and P1/P2 tenants for 30 days. For longer, export them with diagnostic settings.","Monitoring",["30 days is the retention for tenants with P1 or P2 licenses, not Free tenants.","90 days is longer than Entra ID keeps sign-in and audit logs at any license level.",null,"Entra ID doesn't keep sign-in and audit logs for a year; you'd need to export them."],null],["sc82",0,4,"The SOC uses a third-party SIEM and wants Entra sign-in and audit logs streamed in near real time. Which destination should the diagnostic setting use?",["Azure Event Hubs","A storage account archive","Identity Secure Score","A Microsoft Entra workbook"],0,"Event Hubs streams logs to external SIEMs. Storage accounts are for low-cost archive, not streaming.","Monitoring",[null,"A storage account archives logs cheaply for later, but it isn't a near real-time stream to a SIEM.","Identity Secure Score is a posture score and can't be a diagnostic setting destination.","Workbooks visualize logs already in Log Analytics; they aren't a destination for streaming logs."],null],["sc83",0,4,"Who changed a Conditional Access policy yesterday? Where do you find this?",["Sign-in logs","Provisioning logs","Usage and insights for app activity","Audit logs"],3,"Audit logs record changes to directory objects and policies, including who made them. Sign-in logs record authentications.","Monitoring",["Sign-in logs record authentications, not who changed a policy.","Provisioning logs record accounts created or updated in apps, not policy changes.","Usage and insights show app sign-in activity, not changes to Conditional Access policies.",null],null],["sc84",0,4,"Management wants a single score of how well identity security best practices are adopted, with a list of improvement actions. What should you show them?",["The Conditional Access insights workbook","Identity Secure Score","PIM alerts","Access review history report"],1,"Identity Secure Score measures adoption of recommended identity settings and lists improvement actions. The workbook focuses on Conditional Access results only.","Monitoring",["The Conditional Access insights workbook only shows how Conditional Access policies affect sign-ins; it isn't an overall score.",null,"PIM alerts flag specific privileged access issues rather than giving one overall score.","Access review history reports show review decisions, not overall adoption of best practices."],null],["sc85",0,4,"You send SignInLogs to Log Analytics. Which query finds failed sign-ins over the last day?",["AuditLogs | where OperationName == \"Sign-in\" and Result == \"success\"","SigninLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(1d) | where ResultType != \"0\"","SigninLogs | where ResultType == \"0\" | summarize count()","Heartbeat | where Computer contains \"login\""],1,"In SigninLogs, ResultType \"0\" means success, so a filter on non-zero values returns failures. AuditLogs holds directory changes, not sign-ins.","Monitoring",["AuditLogs holds directory changes, not sign-ins, and this filter looks for successes, not failures.",null,"ResultType 0 means success, so this counts successful sign-ins and has no time filter.","The Heartbeat table holds agent health data, not user sign-ins."],null],["sc86",0,4,"Access package assignments for contractors should end after 6 months unless extended, and managers must review them every quarter. Where is this set?",["The catalog's owner settings","A PIM role setting for the catalog","The contractors' administrative unit with a restricted management setting enabled","The access package's assignment policy (expiration and access reviews)"],3,"The assignment policy in an access package sets expiration and recurring access reviews. Catalogs hold resources and delegated owners but not these lifecycle settings.","Entitlement management",["Catalog owner settings delegate who manages the catalog; they don't set assignment expiry or reviews.","PIM role settings apply to privileged roles and groups, not catalogs or access package assignments.","A restricted management administrative unit limits which admins can change users; it doesn't expire access.",null],null],["sc87",0,4,"When a user moves from Sales to Marketing, their Sales group memberships should be removed and Marketing ones added automatically. Which approach fits best?",["A mover lifecycle workflow or dynamic groups based on department","A quarterly access review of all department groups, with owners as reviewers","A PIM for Groups assignment per department","A terms of use policy for each department"],0,"Mover workflows (or attribute-based dynamic groups) change access when the department changes. Quarterly reviews would leave excess access for months.","Lifecycle workflows",[null,"A quarterly review leaves the old Sales access in place for up to three months and doesn't add Marketing groups.","PIM for Groups gives just-in-time activation, not automatic changes when the department attribute changes.","Terms of use makes users accept an agreement and doesn't change group memberships."],null],["sc88",0,4,"Which license do you need to use Privileged Identity Management for Microsoft Entra roles?",["Microsoft Entra ID P1","Microsoft Entra ID Free","Microsoft Entra ID P2 or Microsoft Entra ID Governance","Microsoft 365 Business Basic with the Entra ID add-on for administrators"],2,"PIM needs Entra ID P2 or Entra ID Governance licenses for the users who benefit from it. P1 does not include PIM.","Licensing",["P1 covers Conditional Access and dynamic groups, but PIM needs P2 or Entra ID Governance.","The Free tier doesn't include Privileged Identity Management.",null,"Business Basic includes only Entra ID Free features, and PIM needs P2 or Entra ID Governance licenses, not a generic admin add-on."],null]]);
